114	/* The prompt-box glow ring (PromptHero) — a Gemini-style "chasing light"
115	   ring, rebuilt using only decade-old, maximally-compatible CSS: plain
116	   `transform: rotate()` and `overflow: hidden` clipping. No @property, no
117	   CSS masking — both were dropped after repeated reports that the effect
118	   still wasn't appearing correctly even in a fresh browser with a verified
119	   non-cached, up-to-date stylesheet, which means the earlier @property +
120	   mask-composite combination (both comparatively modern CSS features) was
121	   the most likely remaining suspect, even though I could not get direct
122	   visual confirmation either way in this environment.
123	
124	   The technique: each layer is a STATIC outer clipper
125	   (`.glow-clip-a/-b`, position:absolute, overflow:hidden, never
126	   transforms) containing one OVERSIZED inner rotator
127	   (`.glow-spin-a/-b`, ~2x the clipper's size, centered via inset:-50%).
128	   Only the inner rotator spins via plain `transform: rotate()` — since
129	   it's oversized, its corners sweeping outside a same-sized box (the
130	   original "wide rectangle rotated as a rigid body" bug) never matters:
131	   they're clipped away by the static outer box at every angle. The
132	   composer sitting on top (z-10, opaque) covers the rotator's entire
133	   interior, leaving only the outer few px visible as a ring — no masking
134	   needed for that either. */
135	@keyframes glow-spin {
136	  to {
137	    transform: rotate(360deg);
138	  }
139	}
140	.glow-spin-a {
141	  animation: glow-spin 9s linear infinite;
142	}
143	.glow-spin-b {
144	  animation: glow-spin 14s linear infinite reverse;
145	}
